          "source_quote": "τὸ σὲ οἰκονομεῖν ἀναδέξασθαι χάριν οἶδα εἰληφώς, οὐ δεδωκώς",
          "english_quote": "Hence I know that, in undertaking to oversee you, I have received a favor, not given one.",
          "issue": "The draft makes Peter undertake the oversight of Clement. The stronger contextual analysis takes Clement as the subject of the infinitival undertaking: Clement undertakes to administer, and Peter regards that undertaking as a favor received. The nominative participles εἰληφώς and δεδωκώς establish Peter as the recipient rather than giver of the favor; they do not establish him as the subject of the embedded infinitives. The draft therefore assigns an administrative relationship that the supplied wording does not securely establish.",
          "suggested_revision": "Proposed replacement, not yet accepted: “Hence I know that, in your undertaking to administer, I have received a favor, not given one.” Review this proposal afresh after separately documenting the grammatical relationships among the articular infinitival expression, σέ, χάριν, and οἶδα.",
          "severity": "major"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "τὸ σὲ οἰκονομεῖν ἀναδέξασθαι",
          "english_quote": "A further, less explicit analysis makes Clement the subject of ἀναδέξασθαι as well, so that Clement undertakes the administration, but the change of subject is not overtly marked.",
          "issue": "The source_issues note overlooks σέ as an overt possible accusative subject of the entire infinitival expression and consequently misranks the alternatives. The first-person subject of οἶδα must be distinguished from the subject of the embedded infinitives. The assignment of administration remains an unresolved source-meaning issue requiring separate grammatical evidence. The image-checked print establishes the supplied wording, not the interpretation; no corruption has been demonstrated.",
          "suggested_revision": "Provide a separate grammatical assessment, supported by cited comparable Greek constructions, of σέ as subject of the entire infinitival expression versus object of οἰκονομεῖν. Rewrite the disclosure to reflect that evidence and preserve any remaining alternatives. Do not propose a Greek correction merely to regularize the construction. Keep the passage blocked pending that assessment and fresh review of the English.",
